## 🛡️ 3. The Unlinked Identity-Context Handoff (The Parameter Cross-Over)## The Developer's Assumption:
"The entire withdrawal request is tied securely to the user's active session token. A logged-in user can only route money out to a payment destination owned by their own identity profile."
## The Reality:
When processing a withdrawal, the front-end API gateway verifies your authorization token securely. However, to execute the actual payout, the gateway must pass a data object down to an internal payout queue microservice.
Developers frequently assume that because the data came from the secure gateway, the payload structure inside the nested microservice configuration can be completely trusted without a secondary ownership audit.
## How to Break It:
Intercept the final withdrawal execution payload inside your proxy tool. Look past the top-level keys and inspect the deep nested JSON properties used to pass data to the regional provider:

{
  "session_id": "VALID_USER_A_SESSION",
  "withdrawal_config": {
    "amount": 100,
    "currency": "BRL",
    "payout_channel": "PIX",
    "recipient_metadata": {
      "player_id": 998231,
      "gateway_account_identifier": "attacker@email.com"
    }
  }
}

## The Logic Collapse:
Modify the nested player_id or target variable inside the metadata object to point to User B (the victim account), while keeping the gateway_account_identifier set to your own external account profile.
If the internal backend processing module uses the top-level session to deduct the $100 charge from the victim’s balance, but uses the nested metadata parameters to route the physical money stream out to your external banking gateway, you can systematically siphon funds from alternate high-value accounts without possessing their direct access credentials.
